@@ -0,0 +1,38 @@
+# Batch reservations with optional partial fulfilment
+
+The storefront reserves a whole cart at checkout. Today it calls
+`reserve_many`, which fails the entire cart when one SKU is short. Product
+wants to offer "we can hold 3 of the 5 mugs you asked for, continue?"
+instead, so this adds a batch API that can grant partial quantities.
+
+## Changes
+
+- `Inventory.reserve_batch(items, *, ttl=None, partial=False) -> BatchReservation`
+  - `partial=False`: all-or-nothing, same contract as `reserve_many`; raises
+    `InsufficientStockError` and holds nothing if any SKU is short.
+  - `partial=True`: each SKU gets `min(requested, available)` units (SKUs with
+    nothing available get no reservation) and `shortfall` reports the units
+    still missing per SKU.
+  - Quantities are validated and unknown SKUs rejected before anything is
+    held. Reservations honour `ttl` / the inventory's `default_ttl` exactly
+    like `reserve()`.
+  - SKUs are processed in sorted order so overlapping batches always contend
+    for SKUs in the same sequence (and results are deterministic).
+- `Inventory.release_batch(batch)`: releases a batch's reservations, skipping
+  ones that are already committed/released/expired, so the storefront can
+  call it unconditionally when the shopper abandons the reduced cart.
+- `BatchReservation` (exported): `reservations`, `shortfall`, `complete`,
+  `quantity(sku)`.
+
+Availability is clamped at zero in partial mode because `set_stock()` can
+legitimately push `on_hand` below the units already reserved (stock counts
+after a stock-take); negative availability must just mean "none".
+
+The caller's `items` mapping is the cart; the storefront reuses it to render
+the reduced cart afterwards.
+
+## Tests
+
+`tests/test_inventory_batch.py`: full batch, all-or-nothing shortage on a
+single SKU, partial fulfilment and shortfall, availability below zero,
+`release_batch`, validation, unknown SKUs.
